WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:05.848
There is nothing quite as breathtaking as a pod of humpback whales swimming in the open ocean.

2
00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:15.892
They call to each other with haunting songs, leap majestically into the air, and crash back into the waves, slipping effortlessly into the watery depths.

3
00:00:15.893 --> 00:00:20.731
Many people enjoy observing whales for their beauty, power, and grace.

4
00:00:20.732 --> 00:00:27.277
But not everyone knows that whales are more closely related to humans than to their fish neighbors.

5
00:00:27.278 --> 00:00:37.280
Humans and whales actually have a lot in common because both are mammals: creatures that are warm-blooded, breathe air, and give birth to live young. Scientists

6
00:00:37.281 --> 00:00:47.323
studying evolution believe that, once upon a time, the ancestors of both people and whales walked on all fours near the water’s edge. The

7
00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:53.750
idea that the ancestors of whales lived on land was once a big problem for biologists. Even

8
00:00:53.751 --> 00:01:03.482
now, some religious scholars think the differences between marine mammals and land dwellers are too great to be explained by the theory of evolution. But

9
00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:14.728
recent developments in DNA research and fossil analysis have convinced the scientific community that the physical similarities in the fossil record cannot be ignored. For

10
00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:23.066
example, the bone structure of a whale’s flipper greatly resembles the jointed arrangement of bones in a human hand. Whales

11
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:33.013
also have hip bones, and some specimens even have tiny leg bones—where their hind legs used to be—contained completely within their bodies. But

12
00:01:33.014 --> 00:01:42.491
researchers have only recently been able to compile this evidence and to understand the amazing changes this animal has undergone over time. Over

13
00:01:42.492 --> 00:01:55.108
the past few decades, scientists have uncovered the fossils of hundreds of long-dead specimens that have proven to be some of the "missing links" between whales and their land-dwelling ancestors. With these

14
00:01:55.109 --> 00:02:05.697
fossils, scientists are trying to determine how small, furry land animals evolved into the dozens of species of whales we know today. Scientists believe

15
00:02:05.698 --> 00:02:12.809
that life on planet Earth began in the oceans, over three billion years ago. Slowly, some

16
00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:22.304
organisms began to adapt to dry habitats, until the whole planet—the land and the water—was teeming with life. By around

17
00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:27.499
65 million years ago, dinosaurs dominated the landscape. One popular

18
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:35.731
theory states that the earth changed dramatically when an asteroid slammed into it, throwing up a massive dust cloud. This cloud

19
00:02:35.732 --> 00:02:42.333
cooled the planet, causing the extinction of dinosaurs and many other plants and animals. The surviving

20
00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:46.822
animals could now take over the newly abandoned territories. Within a

21
00:02:46.823 --> 00:02:52.002
few million years, mammals of all shapes and sizes were flourishing. One of

22
00:02:52.003 --> 00:02:59.805
these survivors evolved into a hoofed animal about the size of a fox, which experts named Indohyus . This

23
00:02:59.806 --> 00:03:05.588
whale ancestor probably took its first steps into the water to avoid predators. Indohyus

24
00:03:05.589 --> 00:03:12.920
didn’t look like a whale, but the structure of its bones and teeth was similar to that of modern whales. Within eight

25
00:03:12.921 --> 00:03:20.366
million years, a descendant of this creature had developed an improved ability to hunt in shallow water. When paleontologists

26
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:31.354
working in Pakistan in 1979 found a fossilized skull of this wolf-sized animal, they named it Pakicetus , for "Pakistan whale." The

27
00:03:31.355 --> 00:03:38.239
Pakicetus skull had peculiarities in the bones of its ear that exist today only in whales. Another

28
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.849
species was soon unearthed, thought to be around 49 million years old. The

29
00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:50.618
body of Ambulocetus , or "walking whale," shared underwater adaptations with Pakicetus

30
00:03:50.619 --> 00:04:01.002
. Still designed to prowl both the shore and the nearby shallow waters, Ambulocetus may have had help from webbed feet, short but powerful limbs, and a strong

31
00:04:01.003 --> 00:04:07.049
tail. Rodhocetus , another intermediary species, appeared around 42 million

32
00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.990
years ago. It was the size of a sea lion and could have looked like a crocodile

33
00:04:11.991 --> 00:04:24.691
with fur. By the time of Rodhocetus , the ancient ancestors of whales were spending most of their time in the water, but they still moved the same way that dogs do today: they basically trotted

34
00:04:24.692 --> 00:04:33.512
through water. Eventually, the same forces that shaped fish—those forces involved in underwater movement—began to impact

35
00:04:33.513 --> 00:04:39.691
these mammals. This is why both whales and fishes have streamlined bodies. They use them

36
00:04:39.692 --> 00:04:46.345
differently, however: Whales swim by moving their tails up and down, while fish swim by moving their tails from side

37
00:04:46.346 --> 00:04:52.161
to side. Another adaptation developing during the time of Rodhocetus was a new way

38
00:04:52.162 --> 00:05:02.607
to hear. Visibility was often limited in the water. As a result, these creatures began to depend more on sound than sight to find prey and

39
00:05:02.608 --> 00:05:07.629
avoid predators. The external ear openings of most land animals became

40
00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:13.723
less useful. Instead, the ancestors of whales began to use their lower jaws to

41
00:05:13.724 --> 00:05:20.983
focus sounds. The vibrations were then transmitted through their skulls to the auditory canal, a tube inside

42
00:05:20.984 --> 00:05:25.695
the ear. The next known whale ancestors appeared forty million

43
00:05:25.696 --> 00:05:30.686
years ago. Further adaptations in body structure had made them more efficient in

44
00:05:30.687 --> 00:05:39.932
the water. Dorudon and Basilosaurus had lost almost all traces of their hind legs, and the ends of their tails flared out into

45
00:05:39.933 --> 00:05:44.407
horizontal flukes. Most of their fur had disappeared, making them faster

46
00:05:44.408 --> 00:05:54.019
swimmers. The fronts of their skulls had grown long, and their nostrils had become blowholes at the tops of their heads, allowing them to breathe easily while

47
00:05:54.020 --> 00:06:02.107
swimming. Through this gradual process, a species that resembled a modern whale had appeared by around 35 million years

48
00:06:02.108 --> 00:06:10.219
ago. The adaptations, however, continued, as whales evolved into two separate groups: toothed whales and baleen

49
00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:15.154
whales. The toothed whales include sperm whales, orcas, dolphins, and

50
00:06:15.155 --> 00:06:22.836
porpoises. Toothed whales developed echolocation, a way to use sound waves to explore their

51
00:06:22.837 --> 00:06:34.315
surroundings. They produce whistles and clicks of different frequencies, and by listening for echoes bouncing off objects, they can identify the locations of other creatures and structures around

52
00:06:34.316 --> 00:06:40.688
them. Baleen whales are larger than toothed whales and include humpback, blue, gray, and mink

53
00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:54.303
whales. Scientists think baleen whales lost the ability to use echolocation when they stopped chasing after large fish and other mammals and started eating tiny fish and plankton, which swim in large, slow-moving

54
00:06:54.304 --> 00:07:02.068
schools. The teeth of these whales transformed into baleen—long, thick curtains of hair-like material that filter food from the water.

55
00:07:02.069 --> 00:07:14.343
Although they cannot echolocate, many baleen whales sing complicated songs that can be heard for miles, and are being studied by experts to try to decipher their mysterious language.

56
00:07:14.344 --> 00:07:20.549
Evolutionary biologists continue to study the unique characteristics of these marine mammals.

57
00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:38.070
Every year, more pieces of the puzzle of life fall into place, providing clues about how whales have adapted to their watery environment and changed over time from small, furry land-dwellers to the majestic animals that make their home in the oceans today.

